1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,061
Hi! We're team Coding Claws, at HackGT 13.

2
00:00:03,061 --> 00:00:09,280
I'm Nagur Shareef Shaik, with Sahith Reddy Thummala, Pranav Nagothu and Geethanjali Nagaboina.

3
00:00:09,130 --> 00:00:14,571
And this is Insight Rx. It's a new way to engage HCPs, triggered by a clinical signal.

4
00:00:14,571 --> 00:00:18,746
One simple, no-needle eye photo tells the clinician what to treat,

5
00:00:18,746 --> 00:00:22,565
which protein and drug to target, and who to talk to next.

6
00:00:23,118 --> 00:00:27,285
So, a little about why. I research oculomics at Georgia State.

7
00:00:27,135 --> 00:00:31,429
And what still amazes me is that the retina is the one place in

8
00:00:31,429 --> 00:00:35,859
the body where you can look straight at blood vessels and nerves.

9
00:00:35,859 --> 00:00:37,304
No needle. No scan.

10
00:00:37,154 --> 00:00:41,710
But in most clinics, that photo just ends up as a line in a chart.

11
00:00:41,710 --> 00:00:45,864
We built Insight Rx so it starts a treatment plan instead.

12
00:00:46,418 --> 00:00:47,664
Here's the problem.

13
00:00:47,664 --> 00:00:52,862
Diabetic retinopathy is the leading cause of blindness in working-age adults.

14
00:00:52,712 --> 00:00:55,908
One in four people with diabetes already has it,

15
00:00:55,908 --> 00:00:59,853
and yet about a third of them skip their yearly eye exam.

16
00:00:59,703 --> 00:01:02,124
And those tiny vessels in the retina?

17
00:01:02,124 --> 00:01:06,919
They mirror what's happening in the heart, the kidneys, and the nerves.

18
00:01:07,475 --> 00:01:11,262
Everything you're about to see runs on real patients:

19
00:01:11,262 --> 00:01:16,548
retinal photos taken with a handheld smartphone camera in Itabuna, Brazil,

20
00:01:16,548 --> 00:01:18,413
from the mBRSET dataset.

21
00:01:18,263 --> 00:01:24,061
A primary-care clinician adds both eyes, the patient's age, and their current medicines.

22
00:01:23,911 --> 00:01:26,481
A quality gate checks every photo first.

23
00:01:26,481 --> 00:01:31,813
Then our fine-tuned DINOv2 model finds referable diabetic retinopathy in both eyes,

24
00:01:31,813 --> 00:01:34,404
and shows you exactly where it looked.

25
00:01:34,254 --> 00:01:39,183
On patients it has never seen, it reaches an AUROC of 0.98.

26
00:01:39,735 --> 00:01:46,131
From there, Insight Rx turns that finding into guideline therapy options for this patient.

27
00:01:45,981 --> 00:01:51,221
And it catches two eye-specific safety risks in the medicines they're already on:

28
00:01:51,221 --> 00:01:55,641
semaglutide with retinopathy, and pioglitazone with macular edema.

29
00:01:56,193 --> 00:02:01,579
Now here's the part no screening tool does. Personalized target discovery.

30
00:02:01,429 --> 00:02:05,805
This patient's phenotype ranks the protein targets behind their disease,

31
00:02:05,805 --> 00:02:08,905
by the strength of the evidence, guideline support,

32
00:02:08,905 --> 00:02:12,580
and how well we actually understand the drug-binding site.

33
00:02:12,430 --> 00:02:14,933
And every rank explains itself.

34
00:02:15,487 --> 00:02:20,952
For this patient, the top target is VEGF-A, the protein that makes retinal vessels leak.

35
00:02:20,802 --> 00:02:24,142
Here's its real structure from the Protein Data Bank,

36
00:02:24,142 --> 00:02:27,317
bound by the antibody family behind ranibizumab.

37
00:02:27,167 --> 00:02:32,745
Now let's switch to DeepMind's AlphaFold prediction, colored by confidence.

38
00:02:32,595 --> 00:02:36,292
We compute the 21 residues that actually touch the drug.

39
00:02:36,292 --> 00:02:39,988
And AlphaFold's confidence right there is 95 out of 100,

40
00:02:39,988 --> 00:02:45,485
reliable enough for structure-based design, while the floppy regions get flagged.

41
00:02:45,335 --> 00:02:50,064
The approved and pipeline drugs on this target come straight from ChEMBL.

42
00:02:50,616 --> 00:02:53,676
The same analysis runs for every target.

43
00:02:53,676 --> 00:02:58,110
Here's empagliflozin, sitting right in the SGLT2 pocket.

44
00:02:58,665 --> 00:03:04,565
Zoom out to a whole clinic, and you can see demand for each target, detected from the eye,

45
00:03:04,565 --> 00:03:07,121
against how crowded that drug space is.

46
00:03:07,121 --> 00:03:10,680
That shows a pharma partner exactly where to engage.

47
00:03:10,530 --> 00:03:15,807
And with one click, you get a personalized therapy report and a discovery dossier.

48
00:03:16,361 --> 00:03:22,166
Recruiting trials are matched by finding. Real specialists come from the CMS NPI Registry.

49
00:03:22,166 --> 00:03:25,971
And manufacturers answer questions through a de-identified,

50
00:03:25,971 --> 00:03:28,636
firewalled medical information channel.

51
00:03:29,188 --> 00:03:30,870
So, what makes this new?

52
00:03:30,720 --> 00:03:33,242
Retinal screening tools stop at refer.

53
00:03:33,242 --> 00:03:39,347
Insight Rx carries one photo all the way to the protein target, the drug, and the physician,

54
00:03:39,347 --> 00:03:41,355
for each individual patient.

55
00:03:41,205 --> 00:03:45,079
It checks drug safety against what the eye actually shows,

56
00:03:45,079 --> 00:03:49,153
measures AlphaFold confidence at the exact drug-binding site,

57
00:03:49,153 --> 00:03:54,913
and turns that finding into a compliant engagement for clinicians and manufacturers.

58
00:03:55,466 --> 00:03:59,973
And the return is concrete. For every 10,000 patients with diabetes,

59
00:03:59,823 --> 00:04:03,114
about 3,500 open eye-exam gaps can close.

60
00:04:03,114 --> 00:04:08,000
Those are the ones Medicare and HEDIS quality scores track.

61
00:04:07,850 --> 00:04:12,505
Each physician-read screening bills as CPT 92228.

62
00:04:12,505 --> 00:04:20,254
That's about $30 at the 2026 Medicare rate, or around $300,000 per 10,000 reads.

63
00:04:20,104 --> 00:04:25,134
And about 2,600 people with retinopathy, 500 of them vision-threatening,

64
00:04:25,134 --> 00:04:27,310
are found and routed to care.

65
00:04:27,864 --> 00:04:32,299
Socially, this is a low-cost, no-needle screen that fits in a clinic bag.

66
00:04:32,299 --> 00:04:35,518
It's built on data from a Brazilian diabetes program,

67
00:04:35,518 --> 00:04:38,888
so it can reach rural and underserved patients first.

68
00:04:38,738 --> 00:04:43,682
To be clear, it's a research prototype that still needs prospective validation.

69
00:04:43,682 --> 00:04:45,772
And every line of code is open.

70
00:04:46,326 --> 00:04:48,164
So why should Impiricus buy it?

71
00:04:48,014 --> 00:04:51,739
Because it's a brand-new channel on Impiricus's HCP network.

72
00:04:51,739 --> 00:04:56,732
A clinical signal, not a text message, right at the moment therapy is decided.

73
00:04:56,582 --> 00:05:01,448
Manufacturers pay per qualified medical-information engagement and trial referral,

74
00:05:01,448 --> 00:05:03,406
all inside a compliance firewall.

75
00:05:03,406 --> 00:05:07,117
And clinics pay per screen, which billable reads help cover.

76
00:05:06,967 --> 00:05:13,388
The moat? Validated retinal AI, plus a personalized target engine built on AlphaFold.

77
00:05:13,947 --> 00:05:20,780
Under the hood, it's FastAPI and Neon Postgres on Vercel, GPU models over a Cloudflare tunnel,

78
00:05:20,780 --> 00:05:25,800
Gemini behind a restricted-data guard, and open data from EMBL-EBI.

79
00:05:25,650 --> 00:05:30,273
Insight Rx. HCP engagement, triggered by a clinical signal. One eye photo.

80
00:05:30,273 --> 00:05:34,422
The right therapy, and the right physician. Thanks for watching!

